<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasrullah Khan (emir of Bukhara)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasrullah Khan was emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> from 1827 to 1860, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghit dynasty</a>. He seized the throne after his father's death, eliminating his brothers, and spent his reign centralising power at the expense of the tribal nobility and fighting the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. He became notorious in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for executing the British officers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stoddart">Charles Stoddart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conolly">Arthur Conolly</a> in 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nasrullah-khan-emir-of-bukhara.jpg" alt="Nasrullah Khan (emir of Bukhara)" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khanikov, Belousov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasrulla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nasrullah was a son of Emir Haydar bin Shohmurod, though the sources disagree whether he was the second or third son, a point that bore on his right to succeed. As hakim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarshi">Qarshi</a> he made his name in putting down the Khitoy-Qipchoq rebellion, provoked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>'s oppressive taxes and conscription. After Haydar's death his brother Mir Husayn reigned two months and was followed by Umar. Nasrullah won over Samarqand and the tribes of the Zarafshan Valley, took the throne on 24 April 1827, and freed the population from taxes for several years to secure support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He reigned while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> pressed from the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> from the south. In 1839 he declared war on Khoqand over the Pishaghar fort, took Khojand in 1839 and 1841, and made the khan recognise him as overlord. In 1842 he invaded Khoqand, where the poet Nodirabegim and other members of the royal family were executed, though Ming rule was soon restored. He took the allegiance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in 1832 and succeeded in Marv but failed in Balkh, and he made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> in 1842.</p><p>He centralised power. In 1837–8 he executed the powerful qushbegi Muhammad Hakim on a charge of treason, abolished the post and employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> without tribal ties. He formed a standing army, the sarbaz, of Russian and Persian slaves trained by the Iranian fugitive Abdulsamad, moving 13,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> cavalry to the irregular reserve. He enforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> through an appointed rais and built his religious standing on shrine veneration, visiting the shrine of Bahauddin Naqshbandi before his campaigns. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>'s economy recover.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the West he is best known for imprisoning and in 1842 executing the British envoys <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stoddart">Charles Stoddart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conolly">Arthur Conolly</a>, and for holding, then releasing, Joseph Wolff, who came seeking news of them in 1843. Tsarist and Soviet historians called him the butcher emir for executing his brothers and warring on his neighbours. He died on 20 October 1860 and was succeeded by his son Muzaffar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nasrullah Khan (emir of Bukhara)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emir <b>Nasrullah</b> executed the British officers Stoddart and Conolly in 1842.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bukhara">Emirate of Bukhara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.78,64.43&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Bukhara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nasrullah Khan (emir of Bukhara)"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5291 4162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5291 4162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.78,64.43&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
